    "worker_summary": "The intervention worked as intended. The first search identified the song \"Bette Davis Eyes\" and artist \"Kim Carnes\" but did not contain the specific weeks-at-#1 data that the question asked for. The hook detected this and appended an assistant message prompting a targeted follow-up search. The actor then searched for \"How many weeks did 'Bette Davis Eyes' spend at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100?\" and retrieved the correct answer (9 weeks) from the corpus. The original source trajectory produced an incorrect answer (6 weeks) by relying on unsupported external knowledge; the intervention replaced that guess with a tool-grounded search. The teacher score of 1 confirms the branch answer is correct.\n\nRisks: The detection logic currently checks for presence of song/artist names without numeric data. This pattern may not generalize if the first search already contains the numeric answer (leading to unnecessary message injection). The flag to prevent re-triggering was mentioned in guidance but not explicitly enforced in the hook state; in this case the second activation no longer met the condition, so it was safe. In other scenarios, a more robust deduplication mechanism (e.g., checking a hook_state flag) would prevent duplicate assistant messages.\n\nWhat to try next: Refine the detection to also classify whether the numeric data type (weeks, years, count) is missing relative to the question, and generalize to other entity-quantity patterns. Optionally incorporate a flag in hook_state to prevent re-triggering on the same step. Consider compiling this intervention into the policy for similar multi-hop numeric queries.",
