      "worker_summary": "**Intervention worked:** Yes. The intervention appended an assistant message prompting the Actor to search for the missing entity (\"First for Women\") after the initial search only covered \"Jane\". The Actor then conducted the second search and incorporated the results, producing a correct final answer (teacher score 1). The source (without intervention) failed to search the second entity and concluded insufficient information.\n\n**Why it worked:** The guidance correctly identified that the original question had two distinct entity names and that only one had been queried. By inserting a clear follow-up prompt in the conversation, the Actor was steered to cover the missing entity, enabling a complete answer.\n\n**Risks:**\n- The inserted message format is hardcoded (\"I found results for [covered]. I still need to check [missing]. Let me search for that next.\"). If the covered entity was not actually found in the results (e.g., search returned no relevant passages), the message would be misleading.\n- The hook state flag to prevent re-triggering was not implemented in this run; the same step could fire again if the Actor performed another partial search. In this case it fired only once, but a proper flag should be set to avoid repeated interventions on the same step.\n- The intervention only covers the case where a single entity is missing. If more than two entities exist or the query partially overlaps both, the heuristic may misidentify coverage.\n\n**What to try or compile next:**\n- Implement a hook state flag (e.g., `\"entity_check_done\": True`) to prevent re-triggering on the same step.\n- Generalize to handle more than two entities by enumerating all named entities and checking which have been searched.\n- Relax the hardcoded message template: dynamically identify the covered entity from the search query results, not just the query string, to avoid false claims.\n- Test with edge cases: where the second search returns no relevant